Wait — there's a bug in `place`: `result` contains a copy of each item, and `byId` maps to those copies... but I create `byId` from `result` then overwrite moved's entry with a *new* copy `{...moved}` — that new copy is NOT in `result`, so mutations to it won't reflect. Let me fix: set result to include the moved copy.

Also the collision loop: `for (const other of result) { const o = byId.get(other.id)!; ...}` — but `o` and `other` are the same object initially, fine, but for moved id they differ. Fix by constructing result after byId, or just make result from byId values.

Simpler rewrite:

```ts
function place(layout: Layout, moved: LayoutItem): Layout {
  const byId = new Map(layout.map((i) => [i.id, { ...i }]));
  byId.set(moved.id, { ...moved });
  const queue = [byId.get(moved.id)!];
  let guard = 0;
  while (queue.length && guard++ < 1000) {
    const current = queue.pop()!;
    for (const o of byId.values()) {
      if (!collides(current, o)) continue;
      o.y = current.y + current.h;
      queue.push(o);
    }
  }
  return [...byId.values()];
}
```

This could loop: A pushes B down, B pushes A? collides(current, o) only when overlapping; A already placed, B moved below A so no longer collides with A. When B pushed, check B against all; B might collide with C, push C down... monotone increasing y, terminates. But could B collide with A again? B.y = A.y + A.h, so B is below A — no collision with A. C pushed below B might collide with A? Only if A overlaps C after push — possible! Then we push A down below C... A was the moved item. That would move the dragged widget. RGL handles this by collision direction. For simplicity this is acceptable but could feel odd — dragging down could make your widget get pushed. Guard prevents infinite loop (y monotone? A pushed down below C increases A.y, then A might collide with something else... always downward, so terminates).

Hmm, but pushing the dragged item down is bad UX. Alternative: when resolving collisions for a pushed item, skip collisions with items already "fixed" in this pass? RGL: when moving item, for each collision, move the *other* item. If the other item collides with the moved one after being moved... The moved item is the anchor; others can be pushed below it. For cascaded pushes, an item pushed below the anchor may collide with a third item; that third gets pushed below. A third item can never collide with the anchor after being pushed below the second item... actually it can: anchor at rows 0-2, item B at rows 1-3 gets pushed to 2-4, item C at rows 3-6 pushed to 4-7, no collision with anchor. Since pushes stack downward in order, each pushed item is below the previous pusher, and anchor is topmost of its chain. But multiple chains from the same anchor could interleave. Good enough for a demo; guard caps loops.

Also `collides` returns false for same id — good, moved item's old copy isn't in layout since withItem replaces.

But there's the same-object issue: `withItem` returns [...rest, item] — place then copies. Fine.

Let me rewrite place.

